# Repository attributes.
#
# .gitignore MERGE DRIVER — the fix for a conflict class, not a conflict
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every calibration lane appends its own bundle-ignore block at the END of
# .gitignore, because rule 29(c) [R-SCREEN] / rule 31 [R-RETAIN] require a screen
# or shard bundle to be kept OUT of `main` by gitignore rather than deleted from
# disk. With many lanes open at once that guarantees a textual collision at EOF
# between ANY two of them: on 2026-09-10, 7 of the 8 conflicted open branches
# conflicted on this file and nothing else, and a branch fixed by hand
# re-conflicted within minutes (main took 102 commits that hour).
#
# `merge=union` is git's built-in line-union driver: on a conflicting hunk it
# takes the lines from BOTH sides instead of writing conflict markers. That is
# exactly the right semantics here — .gitignore is an unordered SET of ignore
# rules, keeping both sides is always correct, and dropping either side is the
# one genuinely harmful outcome (that lane's bundle then reaches `main` and turns
# the Class-E bundle-retention sweep in check_registry_payload_parity.py red).
# Duplicate rules, union's only side effect, are a no-op in .gitignore.
#
# DELIBERATELY SCOPED TO THIS ONE FILE. It must NOT be extended to the
# mechanism-matrix shards (docs/codebase-site/data/mechanism-matrix/*.js) or any
# other structured file: those collide on the SAME key carrying DIFFERENT
# verdicts (an `O` row-add vs a tested `R`, or a stale `O` vs a promoted `K`), a
# union there emits two cell lines for one key and corrupts the file, and rule
# 28 [R-MECH-MATRIX] requires a human-legible adjudication of which verdict is
# later. Those conflicts are supposed to be resolved by reading them.
.gitignore merge=union
#
# GENERATED, COMMITTED DASHBOARD DATA (Wave 5C, refactor-consolidation plan §7-G)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These files are machine output that happens to be committed, not source. The
# "Deploy site to GitHub Pages" workflow regenerates the shared ones from the
# per-run parts at deploy time and is their single writer on main; the committed
# copies exist only so a raw checkout previews the dashboard over file:// (the
# bc-data.js DATA_ROOT fallback). Marking them linguist-generated keeps them out
# of language statistics and collapses them by default in diffs and reviews,
# which is the honest signal: reviewing these bytes by hand is never the right
# move — regenerate them and compare.
#
# Regenerate with:
#   python3 scripts/build_manifest.py                 # the four shared files
#   python3 scripts/build_codebase_site_backcast.py   # copy into the site subset
#   python3 scripts/build_status.py --iso <ISO>       # a keeper's status part
#
# frontend/data/backcast/ itself is a FROZEN surface (CLAUDE.md rule 15) — these
# are attributes only; nothing here moves or renames a path.

frontend/data/backcast/manifest.js      linguist-generated=true
frontend/data/backcast/benchmark.js     linguist-generated=true
frontend/data/backcast/completeness.js  linguist-generated=true
frontend/data/backcast/rubric-consts.js linguist-generated=true

# Per-run payloads (scripts/render_backcast.py) and the per-ISO Calibration
# Status parts (scripts/build_status.py). Same category: gzip+base64 blobs and
# machine-rendered verdicts, committed because the deploy's sparse checkout
# cannot reach the bundles they are derived from.
frontend/data/backcast/runs/*.js        linguist-generated=true
frontend/data/backcast/status/*.js      linguist-generated=true

